<h1>Help with your packages</h1>
<p class="lead mb-4">Install with confidence, manage dependencies, and follow every action.</p>
<div class="pm-help">
<section class="card" aria-labelledby="help-0"><h2 id="help-0" class="h4">Getting started</h2>
<details open><summary>What is on each tab?</summary><p>Installed shows plugins, themes and other nonzero package types, including disabled packages. Type 0 packages are hidden from this directory. Select a card for an overview, expandable package metadata, description and README. Install from disk lets you upload and review a package ZIP from your computer. Browse is a preview of future package discovery and currently shows Coming soon.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do I search or filter installed packages?</summary><p>Search by name, description, author or version. Choose a package type and select Search to combine both filters. Type 1 is Plugins, type 2 is Themes and every other visible type is Unknown. The dropdown shows types available on this site. Clear filters restores all visible packages.</p></details>
<details><summary>Where can I read package documentation?</summary><p>Open a package and choose its README tab. Markdown and HTML READMEs in the package root are rendered automatically. Overview contains the description, preview, dependencies and expandable metadata. Package actions remain available on every tab. Scripts, embeds and styles are removed from documentation. Missing documentation is omitted; oversized or unreadable READMEs show a message.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do I install a package?</summary><p>Open Install from disk, choose a ZIP and select Review installation. This reads the manifest and checks dependencies without running package code. Missing dependencies appear on the same screen with their source links when supplied. Confirm trusted sources to prepare them; their dependencies are checked recursively. If any missing dependency has no source, installation stops so you can find and install it manually. Installed but incompatible versions also require manual resolution. When the full chain is ready, review the installation order and confirm that you trust the packages before installing. Installation hooks can execute code immediately. Back up your site and use trusted sources.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I skip repeated dependency prompts?</summary><p>Select Don’t prompt me again for dependency installation to prepare nested dependencies automatically from their listed source URLs during this installation. Missing sources, incompatible versions and circular dependencies still stop installation. The final confirmation remains required. The preference resets for the next uploaded ZIP.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if dependency preparation or installation fails?</summary><p>Review the error on Install from disk. Downloads do not install package code. Prepared archives are kept privately for up to one hour and deleted after successful installation, cancellation or expiry. New packages install in dependency order and remain disabled. If a later package fails, earlier packages are retained; inspect Installed and the activity log before retrying. Dependency downloads require cURL and a public HTTP or HTTPS source, with a 50 MB archive limit, at most 30 packages and 200 MB total downloaded archives.</p></details>
<details><summary>What happens after installation?</summary><p>A success message asks whether to enable the package. Choose Enable package or Keep disabled; both reload Installed. Escape keeps it disabled and refreshes the page. Enabling checks dependency versions, enabled dependencies and AWT compatibility. Changes take effect on the next request.</p></details>
</section>
<section class="card" aria-labelledby="help-1"><h2 id="help-1" class="h4">Managing packages</h2>
<details><summary>How do enable and disable work?</summary><p>Open a package and use Enable or Disable. The current state is disabled as an action button. Disabling is blocked while another enabled package relies on it. The error names the dependent packages. Disable dependents first. Enabling is blocked when required dependencies are missing, disabled or incompatible.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I remove a package?</summary><p>Select Remove and confirm in the dialog. Removal is blocked when any installed package relies on it, including disabled packages; the message names them. Remove those dependents first. Removal deletes package code and resource records while retaining owned storage and tables. It is not a data purge.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why are system controls disabled?</summary><p>Packages marked system_package, or with type 0, cannot be disabled or removed. This is enforced on the server as well as in the interface. Uploaded system packages are rejected because the framework enables them automatically. Install system components through your trusted server deployment process.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do I read a license?</summary><p>The License tab appears when the installed database record includes a license name or an HTTP or HTTPS license URL. A license URL displays a sandboxed document. Some publishers block embedded documents. Use Open original license if the document is blank or blocked. License content is loaded from the publisher only when you open the License tab.</p></details>
</section>
<section class="card" aria-labelledby="help-2"><h2 id="help-2" class="h4">Access and activity</h2>
<details><summary>Who can manage packages?</summary><p>Every PackageManager page, action, log and dashboard widget requires an authenticated administrator with permission 0. Restricted users do not see package navigation or widgets. POST requests use the framework CSRF token; if a token expires, reload the page before retrying.</p></details>
<details><summary>What do the logs record?</summary><p>Logs show the user, action, package, outcome and UTC date, with 25 entries per page. The dashboard widget shows the latest five actions. Failed validated actions are recorded too; unauthenticated or CSRF-rejected requests never reach package operations. History begins after the migration is installed.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does pending mean?</summary><p>An audit entry is written before a package action starts. Pending means the request may have been interrupted before its result was saved. Inspect Installed and server logs before retrying. Code installation and removal involve the filesystem and are not transactional; a failed action can leave partial changes.</p></details>
</section>
<section class="card" aria-labelledby="help-3"><h2 id="help-3" class="h4">Validation and troubleshooting</h2>
<details><summary>Why was my ZIP rejected?</summary><p>Choose a nonempty ZIP up to 50 MB with manifest.json and main.php directly at its root. The archive must have safe paths, no links or special files, at most 10,000 entries and at most 200 MB uncompressed. The framework validates the manifest, AWT compatibility and installed dependency versions. Duplicate package names and existing directories are rejected.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why did an upload or installation fail?</summary><p>Check the displayed error, PHP upload_max_filesize and post_max_size, ZIP support, filesystem permissions and database connectivity. The log migration must complete before package actions can start. Check that dependencies are installed and compatible. An installation hook may have failed after files or records were created; inspect the site and restore your backup or repair the partial deployment before retrying.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why is metadata or a preview missing?</summary><p>All displayed package metadata comes from the installed database record. The dashboard does not read the installed manifest.json. Missing or broken icons and previews use the bundled default images. Restore package files from a trusted release rather than editing database records by hand.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do I upgrade this package?</summary><p>Use your existing trusted deployment/update process to run update.php, which creates the log table idempotently. PackageManager currently installs new packages; it does not provide package updates or permanent data purges. Remote downloads are supported for missing dependencies with source URLs in their manifests.</p></details>
</section>
<section class="card" aria-labelledby="help-4"><h2 id="help-4" class="h4">Administrator CLI</h2>
<details><summary>Where are the package commands?</summary><p>Package commands are already provided by AWT. Use the framework CLI help for the commands supported by your installed version. PackageManager does not register duplicate commands. This package's audit trail records dashboard actions.</p></details>
</section>
